[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Shared Libraries Structure, Global Vars, and Versioning in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins Shared Libraries allow you to define common pipeline logic once and reuse it across multiple projects. By structuring your repository correctly, utilizing global variables, and implementing robust versioning strategies, you can maintain clean and scalable CI/CD pipelines."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Standard Shared Library Directory Structure"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Jenkins Shared Library repository must follow a specific directory layout to ensure Jenkins can discover and load its components properly. Here is the standard structure:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"(root)
+- vars/
|   +- standardPipeline.groovy
|   +- notifyBuild.groovy
+- src/
|   +- com/
|       +- example/
|           +- Utils.groovy
+- resources/
|   +- com/
|       +- example/
|           +- emailTemplate.html
+- ${root}/vars/standardPipeline.groovy"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"vars/: Contains global scripts that are accessible as variables in your Jenkinsfile. Each file name becomes a variable name."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"src/: Contains standard Groovy source classes. This directory is added to the classpath when executing pipelines."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"resources/: Allows external resource files to be loaded using the libraryResource step."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Global Variables (vars/)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Global variables are the most common way to expose pipeline steps. Any Groovy file placed in the vars directory can define a call method, which acts as the default entry point when invoking the variable."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// vars/notifyBuild.groovy
def call(String buildStatus = 'SUCCESS') {
    // Build status can be SUCCESS, FAILURE, or UNSTABLE
    color = (buildStatus == 'SUCCESS') ? 'good' : 'danger'

    slackSend(color: color, message: "Build ${env.JOB_NAME} - ${buildStatus} (${env.BUILD_URL})")
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use this global variable inside a Jenkinsfile, load the library and invoke the script name directly:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"@Library('my-shared-library@master') _

pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building application...'
            }
        }
    }
    post {
        success {
            notifyBuild('SUCCESS')
        }
        failure {
            notifyBuild('FAILURE')
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Managing Object-Oriented Code in src/"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"For complex logic, helper classes inside the src/ directory provide traditional object-oriented capabilities. Ensure your classes use standard package declarations."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// src/com/example/Utils.groovy
package com.example

class Utils implements Serializable {
    def script

    Utils(script) {
        this.script = script
    }

    def getRepositoryName(String url) {
        return url.tokenize('/').last().replaceAll('.git', '')
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can instantiate and utilize this class inside your global variables or directly within a Jenkinsfile script block."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Versioning Shared Libraries"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Versioning ensures that pipeline updates do not break existing downstream projects. Jenkins allows you to bind a pipeline to a specific branch, tag, or commit hash."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Branch/Tag Reference: Appending @v1.0.0 or @main to the library declaration loads that exact state."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modern SCM Configuration: Configure the library globally in Jenkins under Manage Jenkins -> System -> Global Shared Libraries, and check 'Load implicitly' if you want it available everywhere without explicit loading."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"// Example of pinning a shared library to a semantic version tag
@Library('shared-library@v1.2.4') _

pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Run') {
            steps {
                echo 'Executing with versioned library.'
            }
        }
    }
}"}]
